const SEMS=[
{n:1,name:'Foundations of the Trade',goal:'Understand tyres and handle them safely.',topics:[
{id:'s1t1',t:'Safety, tools and equipment',notes:`
<h3>Why safety comes first</h3>
<p>A tyre holds air under pressure and a wheel carries the weight of a vehicle. Mistakes injure people. Every job starts with a safe setup.</p>
<h3>Personal protection</h3>
<ul><li>Safety boots and work gloves for handling wheels and sharp wires.</li><li>Eye protection when buffing, grinding or inflating.</li><li>Ear protection near a running compressor.</li></ul>
<h3>Working safely</h3>
<ul><li>Jack only on firm, level ground. Chock the wheels that stay on the ground.</li><li>Never go under a vehicle supported by a jack alone. Use stands.</li><li>Stand beside the tyre, never in front of it, when inflating.</li><li>Never exceed the pressure printed on the sidewall. Use a gauge you trust.</li><li>Rubber cement and heat are a fire risk. Keep naked flames away and work in open air.</li></ul>
<h3>Basic tools</h3>
<ul><li><b>Tyre levers and bead breaker</b>: separate the tyre from the rim.</li><li><b>Tyre changer</b> (shop): dismounts and mounts tyres.</li><li><b>Compressor and gauge</b>: inflate and check pressure.</li><li><b>Valve core tool</b>: remove and fit valve cores.</li><li><b>Reamer, plug inserter, patches, rubber cement, buffer</b>: puncture repair.</li><li><b>Wheel spanner, breaker bar, jack and stands</b>: remove the wheel.</li></ul>`,
quiz:[{q:'Where should you stand when inflating a tyre?',o:['In front of the tyre','Beside the tyre','Behind the vehicle'],a:1},{q:'Can a jack alone hold a vehicle while you work under it?',o:['Yes, if it is a good jack','No, always use stands','Yes, on level ground'],a:1},{q:'Why keep flames away from the work area?',o:['Rubber cement is flammable','It makes patches stick','It warms the tyre'],a:0}]},
{id:'s1t2',t:'Tyre anatomy and sidewall markings',notes:`
<h3>Parts of a tyre</h3>
<ul><li><b>Tread</b>: the rubber that touches the road. It grips and wears.</li><li><b>Shoulder</b>: the curved edge between tread and sidewall.</li><li><b>Sidewall</b>: the side of the tyre. It flexes and carries the markings. Damage here is usually not repairable.</li><li><b>Bead</b>: a steel wire bundle that seals the tyre on the rim.</li><li><b>Body plies</b>: fabric layers that give strength.</li><li><b>Belts</b>: steel or fabric layers under the tread (radial tyres).</li><li><b>Inner liner</b>: airtight rubber layer in tubeless tyres.</li></ul>
<h3>Cross-section</h3><svg viewBox="0 0 320 230" role="img" aria-label="Tyre cross-section" style="width:100%;max-width:420px;color:inherit" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M62 196C28 135 38 78 92 50H228C282 78 292 135 258 196" stroke-width="10"/><path d="M82 192C56 138 66 92 104 68H216C254 92 264 138 238 192" stroke="#F0B400" stroke-width="4"/><path d="M104 60H216" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><circle cx="62" cy="204" r="9" fill="currentColor"/><circle cx="258" cy="204" r="9" fill="currentColor"/><g stroke-width="1" fill="currentColor" font-size="11" font-family="Arial"><path d="M160 50V22M44 130H12M276 130H308M160 68V100M62 214V224M258 214V224"/><text x="160" y="16" text-anchor="middle" stroke="none">Tread</text><text x="4" y="124" stroke="none">Sidewall</text><text x="316" y="124" text-anchor="end" stroke="none">Shoulder</text><text x="160" y="114" text-anchor="middle" stroke="none">Inner liner (yellow)</text><text x="62" y="228" text-anchor="middle" stroke="none">Bead</text><text x="258" y="228" text-anchor="middle" stroke="none">Bead</text></g></svg><h3>Reading a size: 205/65 R15 94H</h3>
<ul><li><b>205</b>: section width in millimetres.</li><li><b>65</b>: sidewall height as a percentage of the width (aspect ratio).</li><li><b>R</b>: radial construction.</li><li><b>15</b>: rim diameter in inches.</li><li><b>94</b>: load index, a code for the maximum load.</li><li><b>H</b>: speed rating, the top safe speed for the tyre.</li></ul>
<h3>Tyre age</h3>
<p>Find the DOT code and read the last four digits. <b>2323</b> means week 23 of 2023. Old tyres harden and crack even with good tread, so check the age before you repair or sell one.</p>
<h3>Tread wear</h3>
<p>Small wear bars sit inside the grooves. When the tread is level with them, the tyre is worn out.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'In 205/65 R15, what does 15 mean?',o:['Rim diameter in inches','Width in millimetres','Load index'],a:0},{q:'A DOT code ending in 4122 means the tyre was made in:',o:['Week 41 of 2022','Week 22 of 2041','Week 4 of 2122'],a:0},{q:'Which part seals the tyre on the rim?',o:['Belt','Bead','Shoulder'],a:1}]},
{id:'s1t3',t:'Tyre types and construction',notes:`
<h3>Bias-ply (cross-ply) tyres</h3>
<p>The body plies run diagonally and cross each other. The tyre is stiff, tough and cheap, but it runs hotter and wears faster. You will still see it on trailers, farm and some heavy-duty work.</p>
<h3>Radial tyres</h3>
<p>The plies run straight across, from bead to bead, with steel or fabric belts under the tread. The tyre runs cooler, lasts longer and grips better. Most cars, vans and trucks use radials.</p>
<h3>Tubeless and tube-type</h3>
<ul><li><b>Tubeless</b>: an inner liner holds the air and the bead seals on the rim. A puncture usually leaks slowly.</li><li><b>Tube-type</b>: an inner tube holds the air, and on some truck rims a flap protects it. A puncture can deflate fast.</li><li>In an emergency you can fit a tube inside a tubeless tyre. Never run a tube-type tyre without its tube.</li></ul>
<h3>Common categories</h3>
<ul><li>Passenger car tyres</li><li>Light truck (LT) tyres for pickups and vans</li><li>Commercial truck and bus tyres</li><li>Motorcycle tyres</li><li>Off-road tyres with deep, open tread</li></ul>
<h3>Golden rule</h3>
<p>Do not mix radial and bias-ply tyres on the same axle. They handle differently and make the vehicle unstable.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'Which tyre type has plies running straight across with belts under the tread?',o:['Radial','Bias-ply','Tube-type only'],a:0},{q:'What holds the air in a tubeless tyre?',o:['An inner tube','The inner liner','The valve cap'],a:1},{q:'Is it safe to mix radial and bias-ply tyres on one axle?',o:['Yes','No'],a:1}]},
{id:'s1t4',t:'Wheels, rims, valves and TPMS',notes:`
<h3>Steel and alloy wheels</h3>
<p>Steel wheels are strong and cheap, and a bend can sometimes be straightened. Alloy wheels are lighter and look better, but they crack or chip. Take extra care with levers on alloys.</p>
<h3>Parts of a rim</h3>
<ul><li><b>Flange</b>: the lip that holds the bead.</li><li><b>Bead seat</b>: where the bead sits and seals.</li><li><b>Drop centre</b>: the deep well that lets you get the bead over the rim.</li><li><b>Valve hole</b>: where the valve is fitted.</li></ul>
<p>The tyre’s rim size (for example 15) must match the rim exactly.</p>
<h3>Valves</h3>
<ul><li>A valve has a stem, a removable <b>core</b> and a <b>cap</b>.</li><li>The cap keeps dirt out. Always refit it.</li><li>A leaking core is a common cause of slow leaks. Test it with soapy water and replace it with the valve core tool.</li><li>Replace a rubber snap-in valve every time you fit a new tyre.</li></ul>
<h3>TPMS sensors</h3>
<p>Some vehicles have a tyre pressure sensor on the valve inside the wheel. Break the bead away from the valve and mount carefully so you do not break it. Tell the customer if a sensor is faulty, because the dashboard light will stay on.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'Which part of the rim holds the bead in place at the edge?',o:['Flange','Valve hole','Drop centre'],a:0},{q:'What often causes a slow leak at the valve?',o:['A leaking valve core','A worn tread','A loose wheel nut'],a:0},{q:'When should you replace a rubber snap-in valve?',o:['Every time a new tyre is fitted','Never','Only when it breaks'],a:0}]},
{id:'s1t5',t:'Tyre removal and fitting',notes:`
<h3>Removing the tyre</h3>
<ol><li>Remove the valve core and let all the air out.</li><li>Break both beads loose. Keep the bead breaker away from the rim edge and your fingers clear.</li><li>Lubricate the bead with tyre soap or lubricant. Never use oil or grease.</li><li>Lever the first bead over the rim, then the second, working in small steps.</li></ol>
<h3>Inspect before fitting</h3>
<ul><li>Run your hand inside the tyre for nails, cuts and damage.</li><li>Clean rust and old rubber from the bead seat.</li><li>Check the rim for bends and cracks.</li><li>Fit a new valve if it is a snap-in type.</li></ul>
<h3>Fitting the tyre</h3>
<ol><li>Lubricate both beads.</li><li>Put the lower bead on first, then the upper bead.</li><li>Inflate to seat the beads. You will hear them pop onto the bead seats.</li><li>Do not go above 40 psi to seat a bead. If it will not seat, let the air out and lubricate again.</li><li>Set the correct working pressure.</li></ol>
<h3>Refitting the wheel</h3>
<p>Tighten the nuts in a star pattern, then tighten them fully with the wheel on the ground. Check them again after the first 50 to 100 km.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'What should you use to lubricate the bead?',o:['Engine oil','Tyre soap or lubricant','Grease'],a:1},{q:'What is the maximum pressure for seating a bead?',o:['40 psi','80 psi','There is no limit'],a:0},{q:'In what pattern should you tighten wheel nuts?',o:['Star pattern','Around the circle in order','Any order'],a:0}]},
{id:'s1t6',t:'Puncture diagnosis: repairable and non-repairable zones',notes:`
<h3>Finding the leak</h3>
<ul><li>Look for nails, glass or screws in the tread.</li><li>Spray soapy water on the tread, sidewall, bead and valve. Bubbles show the leak.</li><li>If you cannot see it, remove the tyre and check inside. A tube can be checked in water.</li></ul>
<h3>Repairable zone</h3>
<p>Only the <b>tread area</b> (the crown, between the shoulders) can be repaired. A small puncture is usually a limit of about 6 mm across.</p>
<h3>Do not repair</h3>
<ul><li>Damage on the <b>sidewall</b>, shoulder or bead.</li><li>Punctures larger than 6 mm, or cuts and tears.</li><li>Bulges or separated layers.</li><li>Punctures very close to each other or to an earlier repair.</li><li>A tyre that was driven flat. Look for dark dust or scuffing inside.</li><li>Exposed cords or a worn-out tread.</li></ul>
<h3>Be honest with the customer</h3>
<p>If a tyre is unsafe, say so and explain why. A bad repair can cause a blowout at speed.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'Which area can be repaired?',o:['Sidewall','Tread area','Bead'],a:1},{q:'What should you do with a tyre that was driven flat?',o:['Plug it','Inspect it inside, and it usually needs replacing','Inflate and send it off'],a:1},{q:'How can you find a slow leak?',o:['Soapy water','Guess','Listen from far away'],a:0}]}]},
{n:2,name:'Repair Skills and Roadside Call-Out',goal:'Answer a call and fix a tyre on the road.',topics:[
{id:'s2t1',t:'Puncture repair: plugs, patches, hot and cold vulcanizing',notes:`
<h3>Repair methods</h3>
<ul><li><b>Patch-plug (combination)</b>: the proper permanent repair for a tubeless tyre. It fills the hole and seals the inner liner.</li><li><b>String plug from outside</b>: a quick repair, good for the roadside. Advise the customer to get a full repair soon.</li><li><b>Tube patch</b>: for tube-type tyres. Patch the tube, not the tyre.</li></ul>
<svg viewBox="0 0 320 190" role="img" aria-label="Patch-plug repair in the tread" style="width:100%;max-width:420px" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><rect x="30" y="40" width="260" height="34" rx="4" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="30" y="74" width="260" height="10" fill="#F0B400" stroke="none"/><rect x="146" y="40" width="28" height="44" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="none"/><path d="M152 30V100M168 30V100" stroke="#B3392B" stroke-width="4"/><ellipse cx="160" cy="92" rx="52" ry="8" fill="#2E7D5B" stroke="none"/><path d="M160 92V120" stroke="#2E7D5B" stroke-width="6"/><g fill="currentColor" stroke="none" font-size="11" font-family="Arial"><text x="160" y="24" text-anchor="middle">Plug stem trimmed flush with the tread</text><text x="34" y="60">Tread</text><text x="34" y="108">Inner liner</text><text x="160" y="150" text-anchor="middle">Patch (green) sealed on the clean, buffed liner</text><text x="160" y="170" text-anchor="middle">Stem (red) fills the hole</text></g></svg>
<h3>Patch-plug repair, step by step</h3>
<ol><li>Remove the tyre. Find the hole and mark it inside and out.</li><li>Pull out the object. Ream the hole clean with the reamer.</li><li>Buff the inner liner around the hole. Brush away dust.</li><li>Apply rubber cement and let it dry until it is no longer wet.</li><li>Pull the stem through from the inside until the patch sits flat.</li><li>Press and stitch the patch with a roller from the centre outward.</li><li>Trim the stem flush with the tread.</li><li>Refit, inflate and check with soapy water.</li></ol>
<h3>Cold and hot vulcanizing</h3>
<ul><li><b>Cold vulcanizing</b>: a chemical patch and cement bond at normal temperature. It is easy and good for the field.</li><li><b>Hot vulcanizing</b>: heat and pressure from a vulcanizer clamp cure the rubber. It gives a strong bond in the workshop and suits tubes and bigger repairs.</li><li>Follow the equipment’s temperature and time. Too much heat damages the rubber.</li></ul>`,
quiz:[{q:'Which repair seals the inner liner of a tubeless tyre?',o:['Patch-plug','String plug only','Sealant spray'],a:0},{q:'Why buff the inner liner before patching?',o:['To make a clean surface the cement can grip','To make it shiny','To thin the tyre'],a:0},{q:'Hot vulcanizing needs:',o:['Heat and pressure','Only cement','Only water'],a:0}]},
{id:'s2t2',t:'The call-out workflow',road:1,notes:`
<h3>Taking the call</h3>
<p>The first minute decides how fast and how safely you can work. Ask and write down:</p>
<ul><li>The caller’s name and phone number.</li><li>The exact location. Ask for a landmark or a location pin on WhatsApp.</li><li>The vehicle type and tyre size.</li><li>What happened: flat, burst, slow leak, or tyre damaged while driving.</li><li>Whether they have a spare and the locking wheel nut key.</li></ul>
<h3>Keep the caller safe</h3>
<ul><li>Switch on hazard lights and move off the road if possible.</li><li>Passengers should wait away from traffic.</li><li>Do not change the tyre in a dangerous place. Wait for you.</li></ul>
<h3>Decide, quote and confirm</h3>
<ul><li>Judge from the description: fix on the spot, change to the spare, or replace.</li><li>Give a clear price and arrival time, and agree how they will pay.</li><li>Call when you are close.</li></ul>
<h3>After the job</h3>
<p>Record the job, the price and the customer’s number. Repeat customers and referrals build the business.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'What is the best way to confirm the caller’s location?',o:['A landmark or a shared location pin','Guess','Their house address'],a:0},{q:'Should the customer change the tyre in a dangerous spot while waiting?',o:['No, they should wait safely','Yes, to save time'],a:0},{q:'What should you give the caller before you travel?',o:['A clear price and arrival time','Nothing','Only the shop address'],a:0}]},
{id:'s2t3',t:'Mobile kit: what to carry',road:1,notes:`
<h3>Essential kit</h3>
<ul><li>Portable compressor and gauge (keep it charged or on a 12 V lead).</li><li>Jack, wheel spanner and breaker bar.</li><li>Wheel chocks and reflective triangles.</li><li>Plug kit, patches and rubber cement.</li><li>Valve cores and the valve core tool.</li><li>Tyre levers and a portable bead breaker.</li><li>Torch, gloves and reflective vest.</li><li>A spare tube, sealant and soapy water in a spray bottle.</li></ul>
<h3>Organizing the kit</h3>
<ul><li>Heavy items low and secured so they do not move while you ride or drive.</li><li>Small parts in labelled boxes: valve cores, valves, plugs.</li><li>Cement caps closed and kept in shade.</li><li>A smaller kit for a motorbike or tricycle, with the same essentials.</li></ul>
<h3>Check it every week</h3>
<p>Test the compressor, check the cement has not dried, and refill anything you used. A missing item costs you the job.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'Where should heavy kit items go?',o:['Low and secured','On top','Loose'],a:0},{q:'How often should you check the kit?',o:['Every week','Once a year','Never'],a:0},{q:'Why keep cement caps closed and in shade?',o:['It dries out and spoils otherwise','It keeps the cement cold for patches','It is a legal rule'],a:0}]},
{id:'s2t4',t:'Safe roadside setup',road:1,notes:`
<h3>Before you touch the tyre</h3>
<ul><li>Park where drivers can see you, off the carriageway if possible.</li><li>Switch on hazard lights. Put on your reflective vest.</li><li>Place a warning triangle well behind, at least 45 metres. Go further on highways and before bends.</li><li>Pull the handbrake, put the vehicle in gear or park, and chock the wheels that stay on the ground.</li></ul>
<h3>Position yourself</h3>
<ul><li>Work on the side away from traffic.</li><li>Passengers wait well away from the road, not in the vehicle.</li><li>Never kneel or sit in the traffic lane.</li></ul>
<h3>Ground and weather</h3>
<ul><li>Use a flat board under the jack on soft ground, sand or mud.</li><li>Do not jack on a slope. Move the vehicle if you can.</li><li>At night use a torch and reflective gear.</li></ul>
<h3>When not to work</h3>
<p>If the spot is not safe, do not take the risk. Move the vehicle slowly to a safer place or call for a tow.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'How far behind should the warning triangle go on a normal road?',o:['At least 45 metres','1 metre','Next to the car'],a:0},{q:'Which side of the vehicle do you work on?',o:['The side away from traffic','The side facing traffic'],a:0},{q:'What goes under the jack on soft ground?',o:['A flat board','Nothing','A stone'],a:0}]},
{id:'s2t5',t:'Fast diagnosis without a water tank',road:1,notes:`
<h3>Look and listen</h3>
<ul><li>Walk around the tyre. Look for nails, glass, cuts and bulges.</li><li>Listen for a hiss. Rotate the wheel slowly.</li></ul>
<h3>Soapy water</h3>
<p>Spray soapy water on the tread, sidewall, bead and valve. Bubbles show the leak. A small water bottle with a few drops of soap does the job.</p>
<h3>Check the valve first</h3>
<ul><li>Put soapy water on the valve end.</li><li>Tighten or replace the core if it bubbles.</li></ul>
<h3>Slow leak test</h3>
<p>Inflate the tyre, then check the pressure again after a few minutes. A drop means there is still a leak.</p>
<h3>Rim and bead</h3>
<p>Check the rim edge for bends. A bent rim leaks at the bead and cannot be fixed with a plug.</p>
<h3>Decide: fix, replace or tow</h3>
<ul><li>Small tread hole: fix on the spot.</li><li>Sidewall damage or a bulge: replace.</li><li>Bent rim or unsafe tyre: call for a tow.</li></ul>`,
quiz:[{q:'What shows a leak when you spray soapy water?',o:['Bubbles','A colour change','A smell'],a:0},{q:'What should you check first on a slow leak?',o:['The valve','The spare','The wheel nuts'],a:0},{q:'Can a plug fix a bent rim?',o:['No','Yes'],a:0}]},
{id:'s2t6',t:'On-the-spot repairs',road:1,notes:`
<h3>String plug with the tyre on the vehicle</h3>
<ol><li>Find the hole, pull out the object and mark the spot.</li><li>Ream the hole to clean it.</li><li>Coat the plug in cement and push it into the hole with the inserter.</li><li>Pull the tool out and cut the plug close to the tread.</li><li>Inflate and check with soapy water.</li></ol>
<p>This is a temporary roadside repair. Tell the customer to get a proper internal repair soon and not to drive fast on it.</p>
<h3>Valve core replacement</h3>
<p>Let the air out, remove the core with the core tool, fit the new core and inflate.</p>
<h3>Tube repair</h3>
<p>Take the tube out, find the hole, buff, apply cement, let it dry, and press the patch on firmly. Fit the tube back carefully so it does not fold.</p>
<h3>Bead leak</h3>
<p>Clean the bead and rim edge, lubricate lightly, and inflate with the portable compressor. Do not go above the safe seating pressure.</p>
<h3>Always finish with</h3>
<ul><li>Soapy water check.</li><li>Correct pressure.</li><li>Valve cap on.</li></ul>`,
quiz:[{q:'What should you tell a customer who had a string plug repair?',o:['Get a proper repair soon','It is permanent','Drive fast'],a:0},{q:'Before you fit a plug, what must you do?',o:['Ream the hole clean','Skip cleaning','Heat the tyre'],a:0},{q:'What is the last check after any repair?',o:['Soapy water and correct pressure','Wipe the tyre','Nothing'],a:0}]},
{id:'s2t7',t:'Roadside wheel changes',road:1,notes:`
<h3>Steps</h3>
<ol><li>Park safely, chock the wheels and use hazard lights.</li><li>Loosen the wheel nuts a little while the wheel is on the ground.</li><li>Jack at the vehicle’s jacking point until the wheel is off the ground.</li><li>Remove the nuts and the wheel.</li><li>Fit the spare and tighten the nuts by hand.</li><li>Lower the vehicle and tighten the nuts fully in a star pattern.</li><li>Check the spare’s pressure.</li><li>Check the nuts again after 50 to 100 km.</li></ol>
<h3>Seized wheel nuts</h3>
<ul><li>Use penetrating oil and wait a few minutes.</li><li>Use a breaker bar for extra leverage.</li><li>Do not use heat on alloy wheels.</li></ul>
<h3>Missing locking nut key</h3>
<p>The customer should check the glovebox or boot. If it is missing, you may need a removal tool or a tow to the dealer.</p>
<h3>When the spare is flat too</h3>
<p>Repair it on the spot if the tread hole is small, or arrange a tow.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'When do you loosen wheel nuts?',o:['Before jacking, with the wheel on the ground','After the wheel is removed','Never'],a:0},{q:'In what pattern are wheel nuts tightened?',o:['Star pattern','Any order'],a:0},{q:'What should you check on a spare before fitting?',o:['Its pressure','Its colour','Nothing'],a:0}]}]},
{n:3,name:'Advanced Practice, Special Cases and Business',goal:'Be job-ready and run the trade as a business.',topics:[
{id:'s3t1',t:'Balancing, alignment basics and rotation',notes:`
<h3>Why balance a wheel</h3>
<p>A wheel with a heavy spot shakes at speed. The customer feels a vibration in the steering wheel or seat, and the tyre wears unevenly.</p>
<h3>Balancing steps</h3>
<ol><li>Clean the wheel and remove old weights and stones in the tread.</li><li>Mount the wheel on the balancer and enter the rim size and width.</li><li>Spin the wheel and read where weight is needed.</li><li>Fit clip-on or stick-on weights where the machine shows.</li><li>Spin again to check it reads zero.</li></ol>
<h3>Alignment basics</h3>
<ul><li><b>Toe</b>: whether the wheels point in or out.</li><li><b>Camber</b>: the tilt of the wheel in or out at the top.</li><li><b>Caster</b>: the angle of the steering axis.</li></ul>
<p>Signs of bad alignment: the vehicle pulls to one side, the steering wheel is off-centre, and one edge of the tyre wears faster. Refer these jobs to an alignment machine.</p>
<h3>Rotation</h3>
<p>Swap tyres between axles to even out wear, often about every 8,000 to 10,000 km. Follow the vehicle maker’s pattern.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'What does an unbalanced wheel cause?',o:['Vibration at speed','A flat tyre','Better grip'],a:0},{q:'A vehicle pulling to one side suggests:',o:['Alignment problems','A new valve cap','Too much tread'],a:0},{q:'Why rotate tyres?',o:['To even out wear','To fix punctures','To change size'],a:0}]},
{id:'s3t2',t:'Inflation, maintenance and wear patterns',notes:`
<h3>Correct pressure</h3>
<ul><li>Check tyres cold, before driving.</li><li>Use the pressure on the vehicle’s tyre label or manual. Do not use the maximum pressure printed on the sidewall.</li><li>Check once a month and before long trips. Check the spare too.</li></ul>
<h3>Reading wear patterns</h3>
<ul><li><b>Both shoulders worn</b>: under-inflated.</li><li><b>Centre worn</b>: over-inflated.</li><li><b>One edge worn</b>: alignment problem.</li><li><b>Patchy dips (cupping)</b>: worn suspension or an unbalanced wheel.</li></ul>
<h3>Maintenance advice for customers</h3>
<ul><li>Keep valve caps on.</li><li>Do not overload the vehicle.</li><li>Avoid hitting kerbs and potholes at speed.</li></ul>
<h3>When to retire a tyre</h3>
<ul><li>Tread level with the wear bars.</li><li>Cracks in the sidewall, bulges or exposed cords.</li><li>Tyre older than the maker advises. Check the DOT code.</li></ul>`,
quiz:[{q:'Where do you find the correct tyre pressure?',o:['The vehicle’s tyre label or manual','The sidewall maximum','The tread pattern'],a:0},{q:'What does wear on both shoulders show?',o:['Under-inflation','Over-inflation','A bent rim'],a:0},{q:'When should you check pressure?',o:['When the tyres are cold','After a long drive','Never'],a:0}]},
{id:'s3t3',t:'Special cases: motorcycles, trucks, run-flats, mud and sand',road:1,notes:`
<h3>Motorcycles and tricycles</h3>
<ul><li>Support the machine firmly on a stand before you remove a wheel.</li><li>Use rim protectors and small levers. Alloy rims scratch easily.</li><li>Many motorcycles use tubes. Check the tube, rim tape and spokes for damage.</li><li>Keep a smaller kit that fits on the machine.</li></ul>
<h3>Trucks and buses</h3>
<p>They are heavy and run at high pressure. Use a proper truck jack and stands, and never stand in front of the tyre when inflating. Read the heavy-duty lesson before attempting this work.</p>
<h3>Run-flat tyres</h3>
<ul><li>They have stiff sidewalls and can be driven a limited distance after a puncture.</li><li>A damaged one may not be repairable. Follow the maker’s advice.</li><li>Check the pressure warning light after any work.</li></ul>
<h3>Mud and sand</h3>
<ul><li>Use a wide board under the jack. Never jack on soft ground without it.</li><li>Dig around the wheel if it is stuck before you work.</li><li>Lowering pressure helps traction on sand only briefly. Re-inflate once back on hard road.</li></ul>`,
quiz:[{q:'What must you do before removing a motorcycle wheel?',o:['Support the machine firmly on a stand','Start the engine','Remove the seat'],a:0},{q:'What goes under a jack on soft ground?',o:['A wide board','Nothing','A small stone'],a:0},{q:'What should you do after lowering pressure for sand?',o:['Re-inflate on hard road','Leave it low','Add more air at once'],a:0}]},
{id:'s3t4',t:'Truck and heavy-duty tyres, split rims, retreading',notes:`
<h3>Heavy-duty tyres</h3>
<p>Truck and bus tyres carry heavy loads at high pressure. A failure can kill. Use proper equipment and follow safety rules every time.</p>
<h3>Split (multi-piece) rims</h3>
<ul><li>They have a separate lock ring that can fly off if the rim is damaged or the parts do not match.</li><li>Deflate completely before taking one apart. Remove the valve core.</li><li>Never hit a rim that is under pressure.</li><li>Never mix parts from different rim makes or sizes.</li></ul>
<h3>Safe inflation</h3>
<ul><li>Use a restraining cage or other restraint where possible.</li><li>Use a clip-on chuck and a long hose so you can stand to the side, clear of the tyre.</li><li>Never exceed the maximum pressure for the tyre.</li></ul>
<h3>Retreading</h3>
<p>Retreading puts new tread on a sound casing. A factory inspects the casing, buffs it, applies new rubber and cures it. It is not a field job. Learn to judge a good casing so you can advise customers.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'What must you do before taking a split rim apart?',o:['Deflate it completely','Add more air','Hit the lock ring'],a:0},{q:'Where should you stand when inflating a truck tyre?',o:['To the side, clear of the tyre','In front','Over the tyre'],a:0},{q:'Where is retreading usually done?',o:['At a specialist plant','On the roadside','In the customer’s garage'],a:0}]},
{id:'s3t5',t:'When to tow instead',road:1,notes:`
<h3>Tow instead of repairing when</h3>
<ul><li>The sidewall is cut, torn or bulging.</li><li>The tyre has separated or been driven flat for a long way.</li><li>The rim is bent or cracked.</li><li>The wheel cannot be removed or the vehicle cannot be jacked safely.</li><li>There is no usable spare and the tyre cannot be repaired.</li><li>The location is too dangerous to work.</li><li>You suspect suspension or steering damage.</li></ul>
<h3>Explain it honestly</h3>
<ul><li>Show the customer the damage.</li><li>Say clearly why it is not safe to repair or drive.</li><li>Offer options and prices: tow, replacement or another plan.</li><li>Do not pressure them.</li></ul>
<h3>Why it matters</h3>
<p>Turning down a bad repair protects the driver and your name. A customer who trusts you will call again.</p>`,
quiz:[{q:'A bulge in the sidewall means:',o:['Replace or tow','Plug it','Ignore it'],a:0},{q:'How should you explain a refusal?',o:['Show the damage and explain why','Say nothing','Blame the customer'],a:0},{q:'Why is turning down a bad repair good for business?',o:['It protects safety and builds trust','It saves time only','It raises the price'],a:0}]},
{id:'s3t6',t:'Customers, pricing, mobile money and growing the business',notes:`
<h3>Pricing</h3>
<ul><li>Work out your costs: materials, fuel, your time and wear on tools.</li><li>Charge a call-out fee plus a repair fee.</li><li>Keep a clear price list and quote before you start.</li><li>Do not change the price after the job.</li></ul>
<h3>Mobile money</h3>
<ul><li>Agree how the customer will pay before you start.</li><li>Check that the money has arrived on your own phone before you leave. Do not rely only on a message the customer shows you.</li><li>Keep a record of each payment.</li></ul>
<h3>Customer service</h3>
<ul><li>Be polite, on time and call if you are delayed.</li><li>Explain what you did and give simple advice on pressure and care.</li><li>Ask satisfied customers to recommend you.</li></ul>
<h3>Growing the business</h3>
<ul><li>Use a WhatsApp business number and share a clear contact card.</li><li>Put your name and number on your kit or vehicle.</li><li>Keep records of jobs and stock so you never run out of key parts.</li><li>Build links with garages, taxi drivers and fleet owners.</li></ul>`,
quiz:[{q:'When should you quote the price?',o:['Before you start','After the job','Never'],a:0},{q:'How do you confirm a mobile money payment?',o:['See it arrive on your own phone','Trust the customer’s screen','Skip it'],a:0},{q:'What helps your business grow?',o:['Referrals from happy customers','Hiding your number','Changing prices after the job'],a:0}]}]}];
const KIT=['Portable compressor and gauge','Jack, wheel spanner and breaker bar','Wheel chocks and reflective triangles','Plug kit, patches and rubber cement','Valve cores and valve core tool','Tyre levers and portable bead breaker','Torch, gloves and reflective vest','Spare tube and sealant'];
