const MORE={};
MORE.s2t1=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Choose the right method</h3>
<ul><li><b>Tubeless tyre in the workshop</b>: remove the tyre and fit a patch-plug. This is the only repair that seals the liner and fills the injury.</li><li><b>Tubeless tyre at the roadside</b>: a string plug is acceptable as a temporary fix. Advise a full repair soon.</li><li><b>Tube</b>: cold patch for small holes, hot vulcanizing for larger ones or a customer who wants the strongest bond.</li></ul>
<h3>Inspect the inside before you repair</h3>
<ul><li>Look for dark dust, scuffing or a wrinkled liner. These mean the tyre was driven flat and is unsafe.</li><li>Feel for broken cords or bulges around the injury.</li><li>Check the path of the nail. A steep, slanted injury path is a reason to refuse the repair.</li><li>Check nothing else is stuck in the tyre, such as a second nail or wire.</li></ul>
<h3>Surface preparation</h3>
<p>Most failed repairs come from poor preparation. Buff the inner liner in an area slightly larger than the patch, with light, even strokes. Do not buff so hard that you thin the liner. Brush away all dust. Keep the area free of soap, lubricant, talc and fingerprints.</p>
<h3>Cement and patch</h3>
<ul><li>Apply a thin, even coat of cement. A thick coat stays wet and weakens the bond.</li><li>Let the cement dry until it loses its wet shine. Follow the label for drying time.</li><li>Remove the backing only when you are ready to fit the patch.</li><li>Stitch from the centre outward so no air is trapped.</li></ul>
<h3>Cold patching a tube</h3>
<ol><li>Find the hole by inflating the tube and submerging it, or with soapy water.</li><li>Dry the tube, roughen the area and brush it clean.</li><li>Apply a thin coat of cement and let it dry.</li><li>Press the patch on and stitch from the centre.</li><li>Inflate, check for leaks, and dust the patch lightly with talc so it does not stick to the tyre.</li></ol>
<h3>Hot vulcanizing</h3>
<ol><li>Buff and clean the tube around the damage.</li><li>Apply cement and let it dry.</li><li>Place the uncured rubber patch over the damage.</li><li>Clamp the heater on the patch.</li><li>Heat for the time recommended for that patch size.</li><li>Let it cool before you remove the clamp.</li></ol>
<p>Too much heat burns the rubber and makes the tube brittle. Too little leaves the patch uncured and it peels.</p>
<h3>Common mistakes</h3>
<ul><li>Skipping the reamer or the buffing.</li><li>Applying cement thickly or patching while it is still wet.</li><li>Using a patch that is too small.</li><li>Trimming the plug stem too deep or leaving it long.</li><li>Not checking the repair with soapy water.</li><li>Repairing a tyre that should be replaced.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s2t4=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>The 30-second risk check</h3>
<p>Before you take out a tool, stop and look at:</p>
<ul><li>Traffic speed and how far drivers can see you.</li><li>Slope, bends and the ground (hard, soft or loose).</li><li>Light and weather.</li><li>Passengers, children and animals.</li></ul>
<p>Then decide: work here, move the vehicle, or tow. Never skip this step.</p>
<h3>Moving the vehicle</h3>
<p>If the tyre is only soft, the driver may creep slowly to a safe, flat place. If the tyre is shredded or the rim is hitting the road, do not move it, because you will damage the rim and lose control.</p>
<h3>Placing your own vehicle</h3>
<p>If you arrive in a vehicle, park it behind the customer’s with hazard lights on and the wheels turned away from the road, where this is legal and sensible. It then acts as a barrier. Never park it where it blocks traffic.</p>
<h3>Warning triangles and lights</h3>
<ul><li>Walk back facing oncoming traffic, so you can see it.</li><li>Place the first triangle at least 45 metres behind. Place it further on fast roads.</li><li>On a bend, place it before the bend so drivers see it in time.</li><li>If you have a second triangle, place it closer to the vehicle.</li><li>At night add a torch or flashing light and wear your reflective vest.</li></ul>
<h3>Passengers and bystanders</h3>
<ul><li>Ask everyone to leave through the door away from traffic.</li><li>They should wait well off the road, behind a barrier or bank.</li><li>Keep children and animals well away from the work area.</li></ul>
<h3>Jack safety</h3>
<ul><li>Use the jacking point shown in the vehicle’s manual.</li><li>Put chocks on the wheel diagonally opposite the one you are lifting.</li><li>Use a flat board under the jack on soft ground.</li><li>Slide the removed wheel under the vehicle’s sill as a backup.</li><li>Never put any part of your body under a vehicle held by a jack alone.</li></ul>
<h3>Keep yourself in good shape</h3>
<ul><li>Carry water and a small first-aid kit.</li><li>Use a knee pad for comfort and a torch with a head strap.</li><li>Stop if you are tired or the job feels unsafe.</li></ul>
<h3>Walking away</h3>
<p>If the place is too dangerous, it is correct to refuse and arrange a tow or a safer location. No job is worth an injury.</p>`;
MORE.s2t6=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>String plug: extra tips</h3>
<ul><li>Push the reamer in at the same angle as the hole and work it up and down to clean the injury.</li><li>Thread the plug through the eye of the inserter so it is centred, then coat it with cement.</li><li>Push the inserter in firmly until a short end of the plug is left above the tread. Pull the tool straight out.</li><li>Cut the plug close to the tread and inflate.</li><li>Wipe soapy water on the plug. A few small bubbles may stop within a minute. Constant bubbles mean you need another plug or a different repair.</li></ul>
<h3>Advice to give the customer</h3>
<ul><li>Drive gently and avoid high speed until a full repair is done.</li><li>Check the pressure after a few hours and again the next day.</li><li>Get an internal patch-plug repair soon.</li></ul>
<h3>Sealants</h3>
<p>Liquid sealants can seal small holes but they are messy and can hide damage. If the customer used one, tell them it must be washed out before a proper repair.</p>
<h3>Valve core</h3>
<ul><li>Inspect the seat inside the valve stem for dirt or damage.</li><li>Fit the new core and tighten it snugly. Do not overtighten.</li><li>Refit the cap to keep dirt out.</li></ul>
<h3>Tube repair at the roadside</h3>
<ul><li>Check inside the tyre for the object before you refit the tube.</li><li>Run your hand around the rim for sharp edges and check the rim tape.</li><li>Inflate the tube a little before fitting so it does not fold.</li><li>After fitting, deflate slightly, then inflate again so the tube sits evenly.</li></ul>
<h3>Bead leaks: causes and fixes</h3>
<ul><li><b>Rust or corrosion</b>: clean the bead seat with a wire brush.</li><li><b>Old, dry rubber</b>: clean and lubricate the bead lightly.</li><li><b>Dirt on the seat</b>: wipe and re-seat the bead.</li><li><b>Bent flange</b>: the rim needs repair or replacement.</li></ul>
<h3>Troubleshooting</h3>
<ul><li><b>Air keeps leaking after a plug</b>: the hole was too large or angled, or there is a second object.</li><li><b>Hissing at the bead</b>: clean and re-seat.</li><li><b>Slow drop overnight</b>: check the valve and the repair again with soapy water.</li><li><b>Tyre loses air after a plug within hours</b>: remove the tyre and inspect it inside.</li></ul>
<h3>Care of tools</h3>
<p>Keep reamers and inserters clean and free of rust. Keep cement closed and out of the sun. Replace worn tools before they fail on a job.</p>`;
MORE.s2t7=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Before you lift</h3>
<ul><li>Put the vehicle in park or gear and apply the handbrake.</li><li>Loosen each nut about a quarter to half a turn while the wheel is on the ground, so the wheel does not spin.</li><li>Find the jacking point in the manual or on the sill markings.</li></ul>
<h3>Removing the wheel</h3>
<ul><li>Raise the vehicle until the tyre clears the ground by a few centimetres.</li><li>Remove the nuts and keep them together, for example in a hubcap.</li><li>If the wheel is stuck, tap the back of the tyre with a rubber mallet or shake it. Do not hit alloy wheels with a hammer.</li><li>Slide the removed wheel under the sill as a backup.</li></ul>
<h3>Preparing the hub</h3>
<p>Clean rust and dirt from the hub face and the wheel’s mounting surface, because a dirty surface lets the nuts loosen later. Check the studs for damage and the nuts for cross-threading.</p>
<h3>Fitting the spare</h3>
<ul><li>Check the spare matches the vehicle’s bolt pattern and size.</li><li>Lift it onto the studs and fit the nuts with the tapered side facing in, where the seats are conical.</li><li>Tighten by hand until snug, then lower the vehicle.</li></ul>
<h3>Tightening</h3>
<ul><li>Tighten in a star pattern, in two or three stages.</li><li>Use the torque in the vehicle’s manual if you have a torque wrench.</li><li>If you have no torque wrench, tighten firmly and tell the customer to have the nuts checked with a torque wrench soon.</li><li>Do not use a long pipe on the wrench, which can stretch studs.</li></ul>
<h3>Checking the spare</h3>
<ul><li>Check pressure with your gauge. Space-saver spares usually need higher pressure than normal tyres.</li><li>Look for cracks and age.</li><li>Tell the customer a space-saver has limits on speed and distance and must be replaced soon.</li></ul>
<h3>Problems</h3>
<ul><li><b>Seized nut</b>: penetrating oil, then a breaker bar.</li><li><b>Damaged stud or nut</b>: replace it before the vehicle moves.</li><li><b>Stuck wheel</b>: rust on the hub, so use a mallet on the tyre.</li><li><b>Spare is flat</b>: repair it or tow.</li></ul>
<h3>Handover</h3>
<ul><li>Show the customer the damaged tyre and explain whether it can be repaired.</li><li>Remind them to re-check nuts after 50 to 100 km.</li><li>Pack up your triangles, tools and the removed wheel before you leave.</li></ul>`;
SEMS.flatMap(s=>s.topics).forEach(t=>{if(MORE[t.id])t.more=MORE[t.id]});
MORE.s1t1=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Protective gear</h3><ul><li>Steel-toe boots protect against dropped wheels. A car wheel can weigh 15 to 25 kg or more.</li><li>Gloves protect from wire strands in damaged tyres, hot clamps and cement.</li><li>Safety glasses protect from buffing dust, wire and air blasts.</li><li>Ear protection is needed near compressors and impact guns.</li></ul>
<h3>Lifting and handling</h3><ul><li>Bend your knees and keep your back straight.</li><li>Roll tyres on their tread instead of carrying them.</li><li>Ask for help with truck wheels.</li></ul>
<h3>Keep the workshop safe</h3><ul><li>Keep the floor clear of tools, hoses and rubber scraps.</li><li>Drain water from the compressor tank regularly.</li><li>Check hoses and fittings for cracks.</li><li>Keep a fire extinguisher and a first-aid kit where you can reach them.</li></ul>
<h3>Chemicals</h3><p>Rubber cement and solvents are flammable and their fumes are harmful. Work in open air, keep caps closed and store them away from heat and flame.</p>
<h3>Look after your tools</h3><ul><li>Smooth any sharp edges on levers so they do not cut rims or tyres.</li><li>Clean and oil the bead breaker and jack.</li><li>Store reamers and inserters dry.</li><li>Replace worn gauges. A wrong reading is a safety risk.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s1t2=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>More parts</h3><ul><li><b>Steel belts</b> under the tread stiffen it and resist punctures.</li><li><b>Tread blocks and grooves</b> move water away and grip the road. Small cuts called sipes add grip.</li><li><b>Apex</b>: a rubber wedge above the bead that stiffens the lower sidewall.</li><li><b>Chafer</b>: protects the bead area from rim wear.</li></ul>
<h3>More sidewall markings</h3><ul><li><b>Load index</b>: 94 means about 670 kg per tyre.</li><li><b>Speed rating</b>: Q 160, R 170, S 180, T 190, H 210, V 240 km/h.</li><li><b>TUBELESS or TUBE TYPE</b>: tells you whether a tube is needed.</li><li><b>Maximum pressure</b>: the most the tyre can take. It is not the working pressure.</li><li><b>M+S</b>: mud and snow capability.</li><li><b>TWI</b>: arrows pointing to the wear bars.</li></ul>
<h3>Age and condition</h3><p>Rubber hardens with age, heat and sunlight. Fine cracks in the sidewall or between tread blocks mean the tyre is ageing. Many makers advise replacing tyres around ten years old, even if the tread looks good.</p>`;
MORE.s1t3=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Tread patterns</h3><ul><li><b>Symmetrical</b>: the same on both halves. It can be fitted either way round.</li><li><b>Directional</b>: V-shaped with a rotation arrow on the sidewall. Fit it so the arrow points the way the wheel turns.</li><li><b>Asymmetrical</b>: different inner and outer halves. The sidewall shows which side faces out.</li></ul>
<h3>Use types</h3><ul><li><b>Highway tread</b>: quiet and long-lasting for roads.</li><li><b>All-terrain</b>: a mix for road and rough tracks.</li><li><b>Mud tread</b>: deep, open blocks for soft ground.</li></ul>
<h3>Load and ply rating</h3><p>Light truck and commercial tyres have higher load ratings. Never fit a tyre with a lower load rating than the original.</p>
<h3>Motorcycle tyres</h3><p>They are rounded and come in front and rear versions. Many have a rotation arrow, and some use tubes on spoked wheels.</p>
<h3>Fitting rules</h3><ul><li>Match the size and type to the vehicle label.</li><li>Fit the best tyres on the rear axle where the maker advises.</li><li>Always follow rotation arrows.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s1t4=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Wheel measurements</h3><ul><li><b>Diameter and width</b> in inches, for example 15 x 6.</li><li><b>Bolt pattern</b>: number of studs and the circle they sit on, for example 5 x 114.3.</li><li><b>Offset</b>: how far the mounting face sits from the centre.</li><li><b>Centre bore</b>: the hole that fits over the hub.</li></ul>
<p>A wheel with the wrong bolt pattern or offset is unsafe.</p>
<h3>Valve types</h3><ul><li><b>Rubber snap-in</b>: common on cars. Replace it with each new tyre.</li><li><b>Metal clamp-in</b>: used with TPMS sensors and on trucks.</li></ul>
<h3>Inspecting a rim</h3><ul><li>Spin the wheel and watch for wobble.</li><li>Look for cracks near the spokes and bolt holes.</li><li>Check the bead seat for rust and dents.</li><li>Check the flange edge for bends.</li></ul>
<h3>Wheel weights</h3><p>Clip-on weights suit steel wheels. Adhesive weights suit alloys, because clips can scratch them.</p>`;
MORE.s1t5=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Using a tyre changer</h3><ul><li>Clamp the wheel securely. Use plastic guards on alloy wheels.</li><li>Keep the tool close to the rim without letting it touch the face.</li><li>Keep your hands clear of the bead and the mount head.</li><li>Work slowly. Forcing a stiff tyre damages beads.</li></ul>
<h3>Mounting tips</h3><ul><li>Lubricate generously, but wipe off any excess before inflating.</li><li>Keep the lower bead in the drop centre as you push the rest over.</li><li>Check direction and side markings before you start.</li><li>Fit the new valve in the right position before mounting.</li></ul>
<h3>Checks after seating</h3><ul><li>Look at the guide ring line on each sidewall. It should sit evenly around the rim.</li><li>Set the working pressure and check the beads with soapy water.</li><li>Fit the valve cap.</li></ul>
<h3>Never</h3><ul><li>Never use a hammer on a tyre or rim under pressure.</li><li>Never exceed 40 psi when seating a bead.</li><li>Never leave a wheel inflating unattended.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s1t6=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Step by step</h3><ol><li>Ask the customer how it happened and how far they drove on it.</li><li>Find the leak with soapy water.</li><li>Remove the tyre and inspect the inside.</li><li>Check the injury size, position and path.</li><li>Check for earlier repairs and other damage.</li><li>Decide: repair, replace or refuse.</li></ol>
<h3>Signs of a tyre driven flat</h3><ul><li>Dark rubber dust inside.</li><li>Wrinkles or marks inside the sidewall.</li><li>Scuffed or burnt outer sidewall.</li></ul>
<h3>Other warning signs</h3><ul><li>Cracks, bruises and bulges in the sidewall.</li><li>Cords showing through the rubber.</li><li>Separation between tread and body.</li><li>Rust marks inside from broken belts.</li></ul>
<h3>Explaining your decision</h3><p>Show the customer the damage. If you refuse the repair, explain why in plain words and offer the safe alternative.</p>`;
MORE.s2t2=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>A simple call script</h3><ul><li>“What is your name and number?”</li><li>“Where exactly are you? Can you send your location?”</li><li>“What vehicle is it, and do you know the tyre size?”</li><li>“What happened? Is the tyre flat, burst or losing air slowly?”</li><li>“Are you somewhere safe, away from traffic?”</li></ul>
<h3>Triage</h3><ul><li><b>Urgent</b>: unsafe location, highway, night, children on board. Go first and advise on safety.</li><li><b>Normal</b>: safe place, daytime.</li><li><b>Can wait</b>: slow leak where the owner can drive carefully to you.</li></ul>
<h3>Quoting</h3><ul><li>Give a call-out fee and the repair price separately.</li><li>Say clearly if the price may change if the tyre is not repairable.</li><li>Give an honest arrival time.</li></ul>
<h3>Difficult callers</h3><ul><li>Stay calm and polite.</li><li>Do not promise what you cannot do.</li><li>If you cannot reach the job, tell them and suggest another option.</li></ul>
<h3>Records</h3><p>Keep a simple log: date, name, number, location, vehicle, problem, price, payment and result. Use it to follow up and to spot your busiest places.</p>`;
MORE.s2t3=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Why each item is carried</h3><ul><li><b>Compressor</b>: re-inflate after repair. Choose a 12 V model strong enough to seat beads, and keep a spare power source.</li><li><b>Gauge</b>: check pressure accurately.</li><li><b>Jack, breaker bar and spanner set</b>: remove stuck nuts safely. Carry sockets for common sizes.</li><li><b>Chocks and triangles</b>: protect you and the vehicle.</li><li><b>Plugs, patches, cement, reamers and inserters</b>: do the repair.</li><li><b>Spare valve cores, valves and tubes</b>: cover common failures.</li><li><b>Soap spray, rags and a wire brush</b>: find leaks and clean rims.</li><li><b>Torch, gloves, vest, first-aid kit, water</b>: keep you safe.</li></ul>
<h3>Motorbike mini kit</h3><p>Compact plug kit, small compressor or hand pump, levers, wrench set, patches and cement, gloves and a torch.</p>
<h3>Weekly check</h3><ul><li>Test the compressor and its power.</li><li>Check cement and patches have not dried out.</li><li>Check jack and tools for rust and damage.</li><li>Restock what you used.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s2t5=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Work in order</h3><ol><li>Look around the whole tyre and the rim.</li><li>Listen for a hiss.</li><li>Check the valve with soapy water.</li><li>Check the tread, shoulder and sidewall with soapy water.</li><li>Check the bead and rim edge.</li><li>Inflate and watch the pressure to check for slow leaks.</li></ol>
<h3>Soapy water tips</h3><ul><li>Use plenty of foam and wait a few seconds.</li><li>Many small bubbles can mean a porous patch or a worn core.</li><li>A tube can be checked by pushing it under water.</li></ul>
<h3>Types of leak</h3><ul><li><b>Fast</b>: a large puncture or a bead seat leak.</li><li><b>Slow</b>: a small puncture, bad valve or corroded rim.</li><li><b>Sidewall</b>: usually not repairable.</li></ul>
<h3>Decide</h3><p>Fix on the spot when it is a small tread puncture or a valve problem. Replace or tow for sidewall damage, a bulge, a bent rim or a tyre driven flat.</p>`;
MORE.s3t1=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Types of balance</h3><ul><li><b>Static</b>: a heavy spot makes the wheel bounce.</li><li><b>Dynamic</b>: weight uneven side to side makes the wheel wobble.</li></ul>
<p>A modern wheel balancer corrects both.</p>
<h3>Good balancing practice</h3><ul><li>Mount the wheel centred on the cone or flange.</li><li>Use the right weights for the wheel type.</li><li>Place weights exactly where the machine shows.</li><li>Recheck. The reading should be zero.</li></ul>
<h3>Vibrations that are not balance</h3><ul><li>Bent rims or tyres with a flat spot.</li><li>Worn suspension or steering parts.</li><li>Loose wheel nuts.</li></ul>
<h3>Alignment effects</h3><ul><li>Incorrect toe wears the edges of the tread quickly.</li><li>Incorrect camber wears one shoulder.</li><li>A pull to one side needs checking on an alignment rig.</li></ul>
<h3>Rotation patterns</h3><ul><li>Front to back on the same side for directional tyres.</li><li>Cross pattern for non-directional tyres.</li><li>Skip rotation if front and rear sizes differ.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s3t2=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Pressure facts</h3><ul><li>Pressure rises a few psi after driving, so check cold.</li><li>Under-inflation causes heat build-up and can lead to failure.</li><li>Over-inflation reduces grip and wears the centre.</li><li>Follow the label for loaded and unloaded vehicles.</li></ul>
<h3>Measuring tread</h3><ul><li>Use a tread depth gauge in several grooves.</li><li>The legal minimum in many countries is 1.6 mm. Wet grip drops well before that, so advise replacing at around 3 mm.</li></ul>
<h3>Wear pattern causes</h3><ul><li><b>Both shoulders</b>: low pressure.</li><li><b>Centre</b>: high pressure.</li><li><b>One shoulder</b>: alignment or camber.</li><li><b>Feathering</b>: toe problems.</li><li><b>Cupping</b>: worn shocks or imbalance.</li></ul>
<h3>Advice for customers</h3><ul><li>Check pressure monthly.</li><li>Rotate on schedule.</li><li>Replace tyres in pairs where possible.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s3t3=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Motorcycles and tricycles</h3><ul><li>Mark the wheel position and chain tension before removal.</li><li>Fit rotation arrows the right way.</li><li>Check the valve and rim lock if fitted.</li><li>Tricycles carry heavy loads, so check pressures carefully.</li></ul>
<h3>Four-wheel drives and off-road tyres</h3><p>They are heavier and have stiff sidewalls. Use extra leverage and take care with beads.</p>
<h3>Run-flat tyres</h3><ul><li>Check the maker’s advice before any repair.</li><li>Look for sidewall damage caused by driving flat.</li><li>Reset the pressure warning after work.</li></ul>
<h3>Mud and sand</h3><ul><li>Clear the wheel, put a board under the jack and keep the vehicle level.</li><li>Do not drive on a low-pressure tyre for long.</li><li>Re-inflate to the correct pressure once on hard ground.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s3t4=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Split rims</h3><ul><li>Check every part: base, side ring and lock ring.</li><li>Look for rust, cracks and bends.</li><li>Fit only matching parts and the right lock ring.</li><li>Do not inflate until the lock ring is seated.</li></ul>
<h3>Truck tyres</h3><ul><li>Use a heavy-duty jack and stands.</li><li>Use long levers and the right tools for stiff beads.</li><li>Check dual tyres match in size and wear.</li><li>Follow the torque in the manual.</li></ul>
<h3>Safe inflation</h3><ol><li>Place the wheel in a cage or use a restraint.</li><li>Attach the clip-on chuck.</li><li>Stand to the side, clear of the tyre.</li><li>Inflate in stages and check the seating.</li><li>Never exceed the tyre’s maximum pressure.</li></ol>
<h3>Retreading</h3><ul><li><b>Hot process</b>: new rubber is moulded onto the casing with heat.</li><li><b>Pre-cure (cold)</b>: a ready-made tread is bonded to the casing.</li><li>The casing must have no cuts, separation or rust damage.</li></ul>`;
MORE.s3t5=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Questions to ask</h3><ul><li>Is the tyre repairable?</li><li>Is the rim sound?</li><li>Is the spare usable?</li><li>Is the location safe?</li><li>Is the vehicle damaged beyond tyres?</li></ul>
<h3>What to say</h3><ul><li>“I can see the sidewall is damaged. It is not safe to repair.”</li><li>“Your options are a replacement tyre or a tow.”</li><li>“Here is the price for each.”</li></ul>
<h3>Working with a tow operator</h3><ul><li>Keep contact details for a trusted operator.</li><li>Agree prices and response times ahead of time.</li><li>Give the customer the operator’s details clearly.</li></ul>
<h3>Protect yourself</h3><p>Note what you found and what you advised. If a customer insists on an unsafe repair, you can decline.</p>`;
MORE.s3t6=`<h2>In depth</h2>
<h3>Setting prices</h3><ul><li>List costs: materials, fuel, transport, your time and equipment wear.</li><li>Add a margin so the business can replace tools and grow.</li><li>Publish a simple price list: call-out, plug, patch-plug, valve, wheel change, balancing.</li></ul>
<h3>Payments</h3><ul><li>Agree payment before you start.</li><li>Confirm mobile money on your own phone.</li><li>Give a receipt or message when you can.</li></ul>
<h3>Customers</h3><ul><li>Greet them kindly and explain clearly.</li><li>If something goes wrong, own it and fix it.</li><li>Follow up a day later for important jobs.</li></ul>
<h3>Records and stock</h3><p>Record each job and each purchase. Keep a minimum stock of plugs, patches, cement, valves and cores, and restock when you reach that level.</p>
<h3>Growing</h3><ul><li>Give out cards and a WhatsApp number.</li><li>Partner with garages, taxi owners and fleets.</li><li>Train a helper and add more call-out areas as you grow.</li></ul>`;
